(The Science of Effective Studying - Lecture Notes \(Demo\)  1. Why rereading feels) Tj T*
(good but works poorly Rereading a textbook or highlighting notes creates a) Tj T*
(feeling of familiarity. Familiarity is not the same as being able to remember) Tj T*
(something later. Psychologists call this the "illusion of competence": material) Tj T*
(looks easy because you recognise it, not because you can recall it without help.) Tj T*
(2. Active recall Active recall means trying to retrieve information from memory) Tj T*
(instead of looking at it again. Examples include answering questions without) Tj T*
(notes, explaining a topic out loud, and using flashcards. Each time you) Tj T*
(successfully retrieve a fact, the memory becomes stronger and easier to find) Tj T*
(next time. This is often called the testing effect: being tested improves long-) Tj T*
(term memory more than spending the same time restudying.  3. The forgetting) Tj T*
(curve In the 1880s, Hermann Ebbinghaus measured how quickly he forgot lists of) Tj T*
(nonsense syllables. He found that most forgetting happens soon after learning,) Tj T*
(and then the rate of forgetting slows down. This pattern is called the) Tj T*
(forgetting curve. Reviewing material at the right moments flattens the curve, so) Tj T*
(you keep more of what you learned.  4. Spaced repetition Spaced repetition means) Tj T*
(reviewing material over increasing intervals, for example after one day, three) Tj T*
(days, one week and two weeks. Spreading practice out over time leads to better) Tj T*
(long-term retention than cramming the same amount of study into one session.) Tj T*
(This is known as the spacing effect. Flashcard apps often schedule reviews) Tj T*
(automatically: cards you find easy appear less often, and cards you find hard) Tj T*
(appear more often.  5. Interleaving Interleaving means mixing different topics) Tj T*
(or types of problems in one study session, instead of practising one type at a) Tj T*
(time \(called blocked practice\). Interleaving feels harder, but it helps students) Tj T*
(learn to choose the right method for each problem.  6. Elaboration Elaboration) Tj T*
(means explaining ideas in your own words and connecting them to things you) Tj T*
(already know. Asking "why does this work?" and "how is this related to what I) Tj T*
(learned last week?" builds a richer network of memories.  7. Desirable) Tj T*
(difficulties Robert Bjork used the term "desirable difficulties" for learning) Tj T*
(strategies that feel harder in the moment but improve long-term learning. Active) Tj T*
(recall, spacing and interleaving are all examples. If studying feels slightly) Tj T*
(effortful, that is often a sign that real learning is happening.  8. Putting it) Tj T*
(together A good study routine: \(a\) summarise the lecture briefly, \(b\) turn key) Tj T*
(ideas into questions or flashcards, \(c\) test yourself without looking, \(d\) check) Tj T*
(the answers and note mistakes, and \(e\) review again a few days later. Sleep also) Tj T*
(matters, because memories are consolidated during sleep.) Tj T*
